{"diagnosis":"The FIFO's `m_valid` and `s_ready` signals are combinational assignments that read the `count` register before it is updated for the current cycle. This causes a one-cycle latency where outputs reflect the previous cycle's occupancy. Specifically: when pushing into an empty queue with simultaneous pop, `m_valid` incorrectly asserts before data is written; when popping the last element without a simultaneous push, `m_valid` stays asserted for an extra cycle after the queue becomes empty. The fix is to compute the next-cycle occupancy (`count_next`) combinationally from current `count`, `push`, and `pop`, then drive `m_valid` and `s_ready` from `count_next` so outputs reflect the state after the current cycle's transfers.","edits":[{"file":"rtl/packet_queue.sv","start_line":14,"end_line":19,"replacement":"reg [PW:0] count=0;
wire [PW:0] count_next = count + (push && !pop) - (pop && !push);
assign m_valid = !rst && count_next != 0;
assign s_ready = !rst && (count_next < DEPTH || (m_valid && m_ready));
assign m_data = memory[read_ptr];
wire push = s_valid && s_ready;
wire pop = m_valid && m_ready;"},{"file":"rtl/packet_queue.sv","start_line":20,"end_line":39,"replacement":"always @(posedge clk) begin
    if (rst) begin
        write_ptr <= 0;
        read_ptr <= 0;
        count <= 0;
    end else begin
        if (push) begin
            memory[write_ptr] <= s_data;
            write_ptr <= write_ptr == DEPTH-1 ? 0 : write_ptr+1;
        end
        if (pop)
            read_ptr <= read_ptr == DEPTH-1 ? 0 : read_ptr+1;
        count <= count_next;
    end
end
endmodule"}]}
